g_of_Germany_(3-2).svg"}},"french-india":{"img":"/images/states/french-india.jpg","cr":{"a":"Sodacan","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Standard_of_the_King_of_France.svg"}},"franks":{"img":"/images/states/franks.jpg","cr":{"a":"Hans Erren","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germania_inferior_roads_towns.png"}},"federated-malay-states":{"img":"/images/states/federated-malay-states.jpg","cr":{"a":"Unknown authorUnknown author","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malaya_(1896%E2%80%931950).svg"}},"free-and-independent-state-of-cundinamarca":{"img":"/images/states/free-and-independent-state-of-cundinamarca.jpg","cr":{"a":"Milenioscuro","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cundinamarca.svg"}},"french-second-republic":{"img":"/images/states/french-second-republic.jpg","cr":{"a":"Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg"}},"first-toungoo-empire":{"img":"/images/states/first-toungoo-empire.jpg","cr":{"a":"Soewinhan","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Taungoo_Empire_(1580).png"}},"frisian-freedom":{"img":"/images/states/frisian-freedom.jpg","cr":{"a":"Unknown authorUnknown author","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigillum_iudicum_Selandiarum_Frisiae_Transparent_2.png"}},"first-dominican-republic":{"img":"/images/states/first-dominican-republic.jpg","cr":{"a":"BetacommandBot","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dominican_Republic_(1844).svg"}},"french-first-republic":{"img":"/images/states/french-first-republic.jpg","cr":{"a":"Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg"}},"free-imperial-city-of-friedberg":{"img":"/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-friedberg.jpg","cr":{"a":"","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedberg_(Hessen)_Stadtkirche.jpg"}},"free-county-of-burgundy":{"t":"Franche-Comté, the imperial county of Burgundy across the Saône, rich in salt and fiercely loyal to its Habsburg rulers until Louis XIV conquered it.","o":"Otto-William, a grandson of Berengar of Italy, gathered the counties east of the Saône in the 980s and founded the comital house. The county lay in the Kingdom of Arles and so in the Holy Roman Empire, unlike the neighbouring Duchy of Burgundy, which was a French fief.","r":"Salt springs at Salins and Lons-le-Saunier provided the county\u0027s wealth, and a parliament sat at Dole. Countess Beatrice married Frederick Barbarossa in 1156, tying the county to the Hohenstaufen. Through marriage it passed to Philip the Bold in 1384 and joined the Burgundian State, then went with Mary of Burgundy to the Habsburgs and the Spanish crown.","e":"Louis XIV invaded in 1668 and again in 1674. The Treaty of Nijmegen in 1678 gave the county to the Kingdom of France, which moved its capital to Besançon. Its lasting name, Franche-Comté, recalls its separate past.","ru":["Otto-William of Burgundy","Beatrice I of Burgundy","Otto IV of Burgundy"],"re":"Catholic Christianity","la":"French","img":"/images/states/free-county-of-burgundy.jpg","cr":{"a":"Damouns","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Franche-Comt%C3%A9.svg"}},"french-west-africa":{"img":"/images/states/french-west-africa.jpg","cr":{"a":"Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg"}},"french-sudan":{"img":"/images/states/french-sudan.jpg","cr":{"a":"Akiramenai","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_French_Sudan_(1958%E2%80%931959).svg"}},"french-equatorial-africa":{"img":"/images/states/french-equatorial-africa.jpg","cr":{"a":"ComandanteBruh","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_French_Equatorial_Africa.svg"}},"federal-republic-of-central-america":{"img":"/images/states/federal-republic-of-central-america.jpg","cr":{"a":"Huhsunqu","l":"CC BY-SA 2.5","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Federal_Republic_of_Central_America.svg"}},"further-austria":{"img":"/images/states/further-austria.jpg","cr":{"a":"SpinnerLaserzthe2nd","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Austria_(1230%E2%80%931934,_1945%E2%80%932000).svg"}},"free-city-of-frankfurt":{"img":"/images/states/free-city-of-frankfurt.jpg","cr":{"a":"Sir Iain","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Free_City_of_Frankfurt.svg"}},"free-state-of-schaumburg-lippe":{"img":"/images/states/free-state-of-schaumburg-lippe.jpg","cr":{"a":"David Liuzzo","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_F%C3%BCrstentum_Schaumburg-Lippe.svg"}},"french-mandate-for-syria-and-the-lebanon":{"img":"/images/states/french-mandate-for-syria-and-the-lebanon.jpg","cr":{"a":"Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mandate_for_Syria_and_the_Lebanon.djvu"}},"free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg":{"img":"/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg.jpg","cr":{"a":"Holy_Roman_Empire_1648.svg: Astrokey44 derivative work: OwenBlacker","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuremberg_within_the_Empire_1648.svg"}},"french-grenada":{"img":"/images/states/french-grenada.jpg","cr":{"a":"See file history below for details.","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Grenada.svg"}},"french-madagascar":{"img":"/images/states/french-madagascar.jpg","cr":{"a":"Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg"}},"former-shu":{"img":"/images/states/former-shu.jpg","cr":{"a":"Pavo Xie","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L.LIANG.jpg"}},"frankish-kingdom-of-burgundy":{"img":"/images/states/frankish-kingdom-of-burgundy.jpg","cr":{"a":"PhylémonCyclone","l":"CC0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Les_4_commendement_de_bourgognes.png"}},"free-state-bottleneck":{"img":"/images/states/free-state-bottleneck.jpg","cr":{"a":"Skrattar du Fölora du","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Free_State_of_Bottleneck.png"}},"frentani":{"t":"Oscan-speaking people of the central Adriatic coast, related to the Samnites, who became allies of Rome in 304 BCE.","o":"The Frentani occupied the coastal hills of modern Molise and southern Abruzzo between the Sangro and the Fortore rivers. Ancient writers considered them of Samnite stock, and they spoke Oscan. Their history before Rome is known only from scattered references.","r":"Their main centres included Histonium (Vasto), Anxanum (Lanciano) and Larinum, which some writers treated as a separate people. They farmed, herded and traded along the coast. They usually kept apart from the Samnite League and its wars with Rome.","e":"The Frentani made an alliance with Rome in 304 BCE, at the end of the Second Samnite War, and fought for Rome against Hannibal. They joined the Italian rebels in the Social War of 91–88 BCE, then received citizenship and were absorbed into Roman Italy.","re":"Oscan polytheism","la":"Oscan","c":"low","img":"/images/states/frentani.jpg","cr":{"a":"William Robert Shepherd","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Frentani.png"}},"free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-gmund":{"img":"/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-schwabisch-gmund.jpg","cr":{"a":"Pwagenblast, Kreuzschnabel, Beckstet,","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schw%C3%A4bisch_Gm%C3%BCnd_City_Overview.png"}},"free-imperial-city-of-augsburg":{"img":"/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-augsburg.jpg","cr":{"a":"Guido Radig","l":"CC BY 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augsburg_-_Markt.jpg"}},"free-state-of-saxony":{"img":"/images/states/free-state-of-saxony.jpg","cr":{"a":"Madden","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saxony.svg"}},"four-macedonian-republics":{"t":"The four self-governing districts into which Rome split the old kingdom of Macedon after Pydna, a short-lived experiment that ended in revolt and annexation.","o":"After defeating Perseus at Pydna in 168 BCE, Rome abolished the Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia. In 167 BCE Lucius Aemilius Paullus announced the settlement at Amphipolis: Macedon was to be \u0027free\u0027 but divided into four republics (merides) with capitals at Amphipolis, Thessalonica, Pella and Pelagonia.","r":"Each republic had elected magistrates and a council, but contact between them was restricted: citizens could not marry or own land across the borders. The Macedonians paid Rome half the taxes they had paid their kings, the royal gold and silver mines were closed until 158 BCE and timber from the royal forests was banned. The first meris struck silver coins showing Artemis.","e":"Resentment and disorder grew. In 149 BCE Andriscus, claiming to be a son of Perseus, reunited Macedon briefly as king. Quintus Caecilius Metellus defeated him in 148 BCE, and Macedonia became a Roman province in 146 BCE, with the Via Egnatia later binding it together.","re":"Ancient Greek religion","la":"Koine Greek"},"fujian-peoples-government":{"img":"/images/states/fujian-peoples-government.jpg","cr":{"a":"Froztbyte","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Fujian_People%27s_Government.svg"}},"falerii":{"t":"Chief city of the Faliscans, a people who spoke a language close to Latin but lived within Etruscan culture, destroyed by Rome in 241 BCE.","o":"Falerii, later called Falerii Veteres, stood on a steep tufa plateau at modern Civita Castellana in the Ager Faliscus, between the Tiber and Monte Cimino. It developed from Iron Age settlements of the 9th and 8th centuries BCE. The Faliscans spoke Faliscan, an Italic language closely related to Latin, though their art, cults and politics were tied to Etruria.","r":"Falerii was famous for the cult of Juno Curitis, whose festival Ovid described, and for its temples decorated with fine terracottas. Its workshops produced Faliscan red-figure vases in the 4th century BCE. The city usually allied with Veii against Rome; Roman legend tells how Camillus, besieging it in 394 BCE, sent back a schoolmaster who tried to hand over his pupils, and the Faliscans surrendered in admiration.","e":"After further wars Falerii revolted in 241 BCE, just after the First Punic War, and was crushed within days. Rome destroyed the hilltop city and moved its people to Falerii Novi on open ground about 6 km away, whose walls still stand. The old site was resettled in the Middle Ages as Civita Castellana.","re":"Faliscan polytheism","la":"Faliscan","c":"medium","img":"/images/states/falerii.jpg","cr":{"a":"Croberto68","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Maria_di_Falleri_10.JPG"}},"free-peoples-state-of-wurttemberg":{"img":"/images/states/free-peoples-state-of-wurttemberg.jpg","cr":{"a":"David Liuzzo","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_K%C3%B6nigreich_W%C3%BCrttemberg.svg"}},"funj-sultanate":{"img":"/images/states/funj-sultanate.jpg","cr":{"a":"LeGabrie","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Funj_Sultanate_1700.png"}},"faridkot-state":{"img":"/images/states/faridkot-state.jpg","cr":{"a":"Robert Alfers","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faridkot_flag.svg"}},"free-state-of-anhalt":{"img":"/images/states/free-state-of-anhalt.jpg","cr":{"a":"David Liuzzo","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Herzogtum_Anhalt.svg"}},"free-imperial-city-of-leutkirch":{"img":"/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-leutkirch.jpg","cr":{"a":"Flodur63","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leutkirch_-_Schloss_Zeil_-_Leutkirch_v_N_08.jpg"}},"french-protectorate-of-tunisia":{"img":"/images/states/french-protectorate-of-tunisia.jpg","cr":{"a":"This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file:","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Beylik_of_Tunis_(1831%E2%80%931881)_and_Tunisia_(1881%E2%80%931959).svg"}},"fib":{"t":"A Pictish province between the Tay and the Forth, the ancestor of the Kingdom of Fife, named in the legend of the seven sons of Cruithne.","o":"Fib appears among the seven Pictish provinces named after the legendary sons of Cruithne. Its territory corresponded broadly to modern Fife and Kinross, between the Firth of Tay and the Firth of Forth.","r":"Nothing is known of its rulers in the Pictish period. The peninsula was fertile, and St Andrews, known earlier as Cennrigmonaid, became one of the leading Pictish churches, receiving relics of Saint Andrew by tradition in the 8th century.","e":"Fib became the earldom of Fife within the Kingdom of Scotland, whose earls held the right to enthrone the king at Scone. Fife still calls itself \u0027the Kingdom\u0027.","re":"Christianity","la":"Pictish","c":"low"},"federation-of-arab-emirates-of-the-south":{"img":"/images/states/federation-of-arab-emirates-of-the-south.jpg","cr":{"a":"R-41","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Federation_of_South_Arabia.svg"}},"free-state-of-prussia":{"img":"/images/states/free-state-of-prussia.jpg","cr":{"a":"R-41","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Civil_flag_of_Prussia_1701-1935.svg"}},"fidenae":{"t":"Small town on the Tiber just north of Rome, allied with Veii, which Rome fought for repeatedly and finally destroyed in 426 BCE.","o":"Fidenae stood on a hill above the left bank of the Tiber, about 8 km north of Rome, opposite the Etruscan city of Veii. It was a Latin town, though Livy says it had Etruscan connections, and settlement there goes back to the early Iron Age. Its position on the river and the Via Salaria made it strategically vital.","r":"Little is known of its government. Roman tradition tells of repeated wars from the time of Romulus, as Fidenae shifted between Rome and Veii. In 438 BCE it went over to Lars Tolumnius of Veii and killed Roman envoys; the Roman officer Aulus Cornelius Cossus killed Tolumnius in single combat and dedicated his armour as the spolia opima.","e":"In 426 BCE the dictator Mamercus Aemilius captured and destroyed Fidenae, and its land became Roman. It survived only as a village; under Tiberius, in 27 CE, the collapse of a wooden amphitheatre there reportedly killed thousands, as Tacitus records.","re":"Latin polytheism","la":"Latin","c":"low","img":"/images/states/fidenae.jpg","cr":{"a":"Sémhur (talk)","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latium_-5th_Century_map-en.svg"}},"french-louisiana":{"img":"/images/states/french-louisiana.jpg","cr":{"a":"Oren neu dag (talk)","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_flag_of_France.svg"}},"free-imperial-city-of-aachen":{"img":"/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-aachen.jpg","cr":{"a":"Laurens","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_St_George%27s_Cross.svg"}},"federal-republic-of-germany":{"img":"/images/states/federal-republic-of-germany.jpg","cr":{"a":"User:SKopp, User:Madden, and other users","l":"Public domain","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Germany.svg"}},"fourteenth-dynasty-of-egypt":{"img":"/images/states/fourteenth-dynasty-of-egypt.jpg","cr":{"a":"Iry-Hor","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:14th_dynasty_territory.png"}},"federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland":{"img":"/images/states/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland.jpg","cr":{"a":"UnknownUnknown , vector version by Lupin, Greentubing, Fenn-O-maniC and