danelaw":{"t":"Five fortified Danish towns of the east Midlands, Derby, Leicester, Lincoln, Nottingham and Stamford, that formed the core of Viking rule south of the Humber.","o":"After the Great Heathen Army partitioned Mercia in 877, Danish warbands settled the east Midlands around fortified centres. Each borough was the base of a jarl and his army, controlling the surrounding district, in an area later known as part of the Danelaw.","r":"The boroughs were military and trading centres that grew into towns, with Danish law, land units and many Scandinavian place-names in -by and -thorpe. They acted together at times, but there is no evidence of a single ruler. The settlers soon adopted Christianity.","e":"Aethelflaed of Mercia and Edward the Elder took Derby, Leicester, Nottingham and Stamford between 917 and 920. After a Norse revival from York, Edmund of England recovered the Five Boroughs in 942, an event celebrated in a poem in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle. The region kept distinct legal customs into the 11th century.","re":"Norse paganism; Christianity","la":"Old Norse, Old English","c":"medium","img":"/images/states/five-boroughs-of-the-danelaw.jpg","cr":{"a":"Midland_Map_-_5_Boroughs_912_AD.PNG: Robin Boulby. The original uploader was Robinboulby at English Wikipedia. deriva...","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Midland_Map_-_5_Boroughs_912_Ad.svg"}},"federal-state-of-austria":{"img":"/images/states/federal-state-of-austria.jpg","cr":{"a":"User:R-41 for flag with coat of arms. Original uploader of the coat of arms was David Liuzzo at de.wikipedia","l":"CC BY-SA 3.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:State_flag_of_Austria_(1934%E2%80%931938).svg"}},"free-imperial-city-of-nordhausen":{"img":"/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-nordhausen.jpg","cr":{"a":"Vincent Eisfeld","l":"CC BY-SA 4.0","s":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nordhausen_-_inner_city_by_Vincent_Eisfeld.jpg"}},"free-imperial-city-of-weissenburg":{"im